{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>For in order to try you has Elohim come</b> — to exalt you in the world, so that a name goes forth for you among the nations, that it is in His glory that He was revealed upon you. <b>נַסּוֹת</b> (to try) — Rashi explained it (Rashi on Exodus 20:17) as a term of raising and greatness, like \"הָרִימוּ נֵס\" (lift up a banner) (Isaiah 62:10), \"כַּנֵּס עַל הַגִּבְעָה\" (a banner upon the hill) (Isaiah 30:17), which stands erect. But this is not correct. Rather, it is possible that it says: for <b>in order to</b> accustom you to faith in Him <b>has Elohim come</b>, for since He showed you the revelation of the Shekhinah, His faith enters your hearts to cleave to Him, and your souls will never depart from it; and <b>so that His fear will be upon your faces</b> — when you see that He alone is Elohim in heaven and on earth, you will fear Him with great fear. Or it may say: that the fear of this great fire will be upon your faces, and you will not sin out of your fear of it. And the expression would be from \"he prevailed to go, for he had not tried it, and David said to Saul, I cannot go in these, for I have not tried them\" (I Samuel 17:39), in the sense of practice.<br><br>And the Rav said in the Guide for the Perplexed (III:24) that He said to them <b>do not fear</b>, for this which you saw was so that when YHWH your Elohim tries you, to make known the value of your faith, and He sends you a false prophet who wishes to overturn what you heard, your footing will never falter from the way of truth, for you have already seen the truth with your own eyes. And if so, the verse means: in order that He will be able to try you in the future, <b>Elohim has come</b> now, so that you will stand firm for Him in every trial. But in my opinion it is an actual trial (נִסָּיוֹן מַמָּשׁ) — it says: behold, Elohim wished to try you whether you will keep His commandments, for He has removed from your hearts all doubt; and from now on He will see whether you love Him and whether you desire Him and His commandments. And likewise every expression of נִסָּיוֹן (trial) means examination/testing, as in \"to go in them, for I have not tried them\" (I Samuel 17:39) — I have never tested myself to go in them. And it is possible that this trial may be for good, for sometimes a master may test his servant with hard labor to know whether he will bear it out of love for him, and sometimes he may bestow good upon him to know whether he will repay him for the goodness he did with him by adding service and honor to his master. This is as the Sages said (Shemot Rabbah 31:8): Happy is the man who stands in his trials, for there is no creature that the Holy One, blessed be He, does not test — the rich one, He tests him whether his hand will be open to the poor; the poor one, He tests him whether he can accept suffering, and so forth. Therefore Scripture says: Elohim did good to you, to show you His glory which He did not do for any nation, in order to try you whether you will repay Him for the goodness He did with you by becoming for Him a people of inheritance — as it says \"Do you thus repay YHWH?\" (Deuteronomy 32:6), and it says \"You only have I known of all the families of the earth; therefore I will visit upon you all your iniquities\" (Amos 3:2), for the other nations are not obligated to Me as you are, whom I have known face to face."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:12:32.554022244Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}